window.MR.regions={"_meta":{"captured":"2026-09-26","what_this_is":"24 remote and cold places, most running on diesel, where a 1-20 MW reactor would compete: what each pays, what it draws, and where its law stands on civil nuclear power.","method":"Two research passes (data/research/2026-09-26-refresh/north.json and islands.json), one record per jurisdiction, each source fetched; the group, the law-and-policy tag and the short price label are curated in that folder's integrate_regions.py from each record's own text. Military bases appear only as context.","groups":[{"id":"alaska","label":"Alaska"},{"id":"canada-north","label":"Canada's north"},{"id":"arctic-europe","label":"Greenland, the Nordics and polar stations"},{"id":"us-islands","label":"US islands, territories and freely associated states"},{"id":"australia","label":"Australia"},{"id":"uk-allies","label":"UK, its territories and New Zealand"}],"position_tags":[{"id":"enabling","label":"Enabling law","gloss":"A statute or regulation already provides for siting a microreactor here."},{"id":"study","label":"Studying","gloss":"A government or utility is studying or planning nuclear power, with no enabling law yet."},{"id":"review","label":"Ban under review","gloss":"A standing ban or restriction on nuclear power is under formal review."},{"id":"silent","label":"No stated position","gloss":"No law, study or official statement on civil nuclear power was found."},{"id":"proposed-ban","label":"Ban proposed","gloss":"A bill to prohibit nuclear power has been introduced but not enacted."},{"id":"restricted","label":"Restricted","gloss":"A legal barrier short of an outright ban, such as a supermajority vote."},{"id":"banned","label":"Banned","gloss":"Civil nuclear power is prohibited by statute."}]},"regions":[{"id":"alaska-legal-framework","region":"Alaska (statewide legal/regulatory framework)","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US state","power_system":"Not a utility record; covers the state-level rules that apply to every Alaska record above. Alaska Statute 18.45 (as amended by SB 177, 2022, defining a microreactor as no more than 50 MW) and its implementing regulation 18 AAC 87 govern microreactor siting permits statewide.","nuclear_position":"SB 177, signed by Governor Dunleavy in 2022, defines a microreactor as an advanced reactor of no more than 50 MW and removes the prior requirement that the legislature designate land before DEC can issue a siting permit (legislative designation is still required only for sites in an unorganized borough). DEC's implementing regulation, 18 AAC 87 (Nuclear Facility Siting), took effect August 23, 2023, and DEC's own regulation tracker shows it 'up to date' with no amendment since, through 2025-2026: applicants need a DEC siting permit plus municipal/borough (or legislative, if unorganized) approval, while the NRC retains licensing, construction, operation, safety and security authority.","microreactor_activity":"An Alaska Center for Energy and Power (ACEP)/Strategies 360 public-opinion survey, presented to the Alaska House Resources Committee on April 24, 2026, found awareness of advanced/microreactors rose from 'about a quarter' of respondents in 2023 to 'about 40%' in 2026, and that giving survey respondents 'a brief, neutral description of microreactors... increased measured support by about 10 percentage points.' No new nuclear-specific bill was found introduced in the 2025-2026 (34th) Legislature beyond SB 177's continuing regulatory implementation.","microreactor_read":"Alaska's enabling law is the most settled of any region in this file: a siting statute and an implementing regulation have both been in force, unchanged, since August 2023, and public support is measurably rising. The gate on any Alaska deployment is now a utility's own commercial decision, not state law.","group":"alaska","position":"enabling","price_short":"statewide framework","loads":[],"blockers":["No 2026 legislative update found beyond SB 177 (2022) and its 2023 implementing regulation; the framework is settled but not further expanded","Public awareness of microreactors, while rising, was still only ~40% as of the April 2026 survey"],"sources":[{"label":"Alaska Dept. of Environmental Conservation — 18 AAC 87 Nuclear Facility Siting (regulation tracker)","url":"https://dec.alaska.gov/commish/regulations/18-aac-87","quote":"DEC Last Change Effective Date August 23, 2023","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Citizen Portal (Alaska Legislature beat) — Awareness of advanced nuclear rises in Alaska; microreactor descriptions increase measured support (2026-04-24 hearing)","url":"https://citizenportal.ai/articles/7879228/alaska/2026-legislature-alaska/awareness-of-advanced-nuclear-rises-in-alaska-microreactor-descriptions-increase-measured-support","quote":"In 2023 only about a quarter of respondents had heard of these (advanced reactors); by our most recent survey that went up to about 40%","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"alaska-railbelt-utilities","region":"Alaska Railbelt","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US state","power_system":"Six interconnected utilities (Golden Valley Electric Association/GVEA, Chugach Electric, Matanuska Electric, Homer Electric, Anchorage's municipal successor, and the City of Seward) share the Railbelt grid from Fairbanks to the Kenai Peninsula. GVEA generated more than 80% of its power from fossil fuels (coal, diesel and naphtha) as of May 2025 reporting. Its largest customer, the Fort Knox gold mine, draws about 16% of GVEA's total output; Northern Star's Pogo mine draws a grid load its own 2025 corporate report labels 'Pogo GRID 16 MW'.","price":"Governor Dunleavy's stated (unmet) Railbelt target is 10 cents/kWh; GVEA burns roughly 200,000 tons of Usibelli coal a year under a contract signed in August [2024]","nuclear_position":"No state ban. SB 177 (2022) and DEC's 18 AAC 87 siting regulation (effective 2023) already permit microreactor siting statewide, Railbelt included (see alaska-legal-framework). No Railbelt utility has itself taken a formal board position for or against a reactor.","microreactor_activity":"May 2026: the Alaska Center for Energy and Power organized a tour of Idaho National Laboratory for utility executives including staff from Golden Valley Electric Association, to view Radiant's Kaleidos microreactor test facility and Oklo's 75 MW Aurora construction site. ACEP's own account of the trip raises, as an open question rather than a utility commitment, whether Oklo's 5 MW Eielson microreactor could be followed by 'a larger 75 MW reactor at the same location supplying power to the GVEA's grid.' No RFI, site-permit application, or GVEA board vote on nuclear was found.","microreactor_read":"The Railbelt's own energy establishment (ACEP) is already treating a reactor at Eielson-scale-and-up as a live conversation because Fort Knox and Pogo prove 16 MW+ industrial loads exist on this grid; the missing piece is a utility RFI or site permit, not the enabling law, which has existed since 2023.","group":"alaska","position":"enabling","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[{"name":"Fort Knox mine (Kinross)","mw":"16% of GVEA's total power output; $40 million/year","note":"grid-connected via GVEA, not diesel; largest single customer on the Railbelt"},{"name":"Pogo mine (Northern Star)","mw":"16 MW","note":"grid-connected; labeled 'Pogo GRID 16 MW' in Northern Star's own 2025 corporate report"}],"blockers":["No Railbelt utility has issued an RFI or committed capital to a reactor study","A Cook Inlet natural gas shortage and a federal freeze on GVEA's roughly $500 million renewables funding package (as of May 2025) are consuming utility bandwidth ahead of any nuclear decision","GVEA reversed a 2022 board vote to close a coal plant and instead signed a new Usibelli coal-supply contract"],"sources":[{"label":"Alaska Beacon — Could nuclear power Alaska's future? (2026)","url":"https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/07/01/could-nuclear-power-alaskas-future/","quote":"Oklo was a familiar name to many of the tour participants, particularly those from Golden Valley Electric Association.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Anchorage Daily News / Northern Journal — A key player in moving Interior Alaska off fossil fuels? (2025)","url":"https://www.adn.com/business-economy/energy/2025/05/03/a-key-player-in-moving-interior-alaska-off-fossil-fuels-multibillion-dollar-mining-corporations/","quote":"The sprawling Fort Knox mine consumes $40 million worth of power every year","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"alaska-mines-power","region":"Alaska mines (non-military civilian loads)","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US state","power_system":"Alaska's large mines split between grid-tied and diesel-only. Kensington (Coeur Alaska, underground gold, Lynn Canal) runs entirely on-site diesel: 'annual fuel consumption expected to be around 3 million gallons at full production.' Greens Creek (Hecla, Admiralty Island) has purchased surplus hydropower from Alaska Electric Light & Power since 2006, and 'since 2009, hydropower has provided 77% of Greens Creek's energy needs,' though in 2025 low precipitation cut that back and '62% of the mine's electricity [was] purchased from the grid' (i.e. thermal-backed). Fort Knox and Pogo are grid-tied to the Railbelt (see alaska-railbelt-utilities). Donlin Gold (not yet built) would need a 220 MW dual-fuel plant and a 316-mile gas pipeline. Graphite One's Graphite Creek project forecasts a 12.5 MW draw, twice Nome's own load.","price":"Fort Knox: $40 million/year, ~16% of GVEA's output (see alaska-railbelt-utilities); no per-mine $/MWh diesel figure was found for Kensington, Greens Creek or Red Dog","nuclear_position":"No mine-specific ban; SB 177 (2022) and DEC's 18 AAC 87 apply statewide. No mine has filed for a reactor siting permit.","microreactor_activity":"Graphite One's own SVP of Operations named nuclear power as an option for the Graphite Creek mine in April 2025 reporting: 'The nuclear is very favorable because it's got no carbon footprint, but they're not far enough along yet, and they've got to be socially acceptable.' This is the clearest on-record civilian-mine interest in nuclear power in Alaska outside Red Dog. No site study, RFI or vendor engagement was found beyond that statement.","microreactor_read":"Graphite One is the cleanest small-mine microreactor lead in Alaska after Red Dog: a company executive has already put nuclear on the list of options at a forecast 12.5 MW load, conditioned only on vendor maturity and social acceptability, not on cost or law.","group":"alaska","position":"enabling","price_short":"Fort Knox buys $40M of power a year","loads":[{"name":"Kensington mine (Coeur Alaska)","mw":"~3 million gallons diesel/year at full production","note":"off-grid, diesel-only"},{"name":"Greens Creek mine (Hecla)","mw":"77% hydropower since 2009 (62% grid-thermal in 2025 low-water year)","note":"purchases surplus AEL&P hydropower; diesel backup only"},{"name":"Donlin Gold (pre-construction)","mw":"220 MW planned dual-fuel plant, 316-mile gas pipeline","note":"not yet financed/built; would displace ~76 million gallons/year of diesel"},{"name":"Graphite One / Graphite Creek","mw":"12.5 MW forecast","note":"pre-production (targeting 2030); diesel gensets are the 'leading proposal' but nuclear is under explicit consideration"},{"name":"Red Dog mine (Teck/NANA)","mw":"~25 MW, ~40,000 gallons diesel/day","note":"see the red-dog answer record for 2026 updates"}],"blockers":["Graphite One: reactor vendors judged 'not far enough along yet' and a stated social-acceptability bar, by the company's own account","Kensington and Greens Creek already have a working power solution (diesel at full efficiency, or discounted hydropower) reducing urgency","Donlin remains unbuilt and unfinanced, so its 220 MW load is not yet a live near-term buyer"],"sources":[{"label":"Ground Truth Alaska — Kensington Gold Mine","url":"https://groundtruthalaska.org/articles/KensingtonGoldMine/","quote":"annual fuel consumption expected to be around 3 million gallons at full production","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Hecla Mining — Greens Creek, Alaska","url":"https://www.hecla.com/operations/greens-creek-alaska","quote":"Since 2009, hydropower has provided 77% of Greens Creek's energy needs.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"KNOM Radio Mission — Graphite One forecasts $43 billion in revenue from proposed Seward Peninsula mine (2025)","url":"https://knom.org/2025/04/30/graphite-one-forecasts-43-billion-in-revenue-from-proposed-seward-peninsula-mine/","quote":"The nuclear is very favorable because it's got no carbon footprint, but they're not far enough along yet","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"alaska-rural-pce-communities","region":"Rural Alaska (Power Cost Equalization communities)","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US state","power_system":"Alaska Village Electric Cooperative (AVEC) alone serves 58 communities off 46 separate diesel power plants, each an isolated microgrid with no interconnection between villages; Nome, Kotzebue, Bethel and Utqiagvik each have their own separate municipal or cooperative diesel utility outside AVEC. AVEC adjusts customer bills every few months through an RCA-approved Cost of Power Adjustment (COPA) filing layered on top of a base Energy Charge.","price":"AVEC's 'All Other' service-area base residential Energy Charge for the first 750 kWh (before the COPA fuel surcharge and before any PCE subsidy): $0.3647/kWh, proposed to rise to $0.3720/kWh in a filing posted 2026-08-08; the statewide FY2026 PCE base rate (the subsidized floor the state pays down to) is reported at 20.38 cents/kWh","nuclear_position":"No PCE-community utility has taken a position on nuclear power. State law (SB 177, 2022) and DEC's siting regulation (18 AAC 87, effective 2023) apply statewide and are not community-specific.","microreactor_activity":"None found at any individual PCE community. Searched AVEC's own news posts and RCA filing notices; found only diesel/COPA rate mechanics and PCE-funding advocacy testimony to the legislature, no reactor studies.","microreactor_read":"Rural PCE villages carry the sector's best-regulated diesel price signal (a published base rate plus a disclosed fuel-surcharge mechanism) but the worst-matched load; a reactor needs a hub-and-aggregation buyer like the tribal utility in the tanana-chiefs prospect, not any single PCE village.","group":"alaska","position":"enabling","price_short":"$0.3647/kWh base rate (AVEC)","loads":[{"name":"AVEC system-wide","mw":"46 diesel power plants across 58 communities","note":"off-grid; no interconnection between communities"}],"blockers":["Most individual PCE villages draw well under 1 MW, far below any current microreactor's minimum output","58 separate diesel microgrids mean a reactor would have to be sited at a hub community and would still leave most villages on diesel","AVEC posted an operating loss in 2025 by its own reporting, leaving no obvious capital sponsor for new generation"],"sources":[{"label":"Alaska Village Electric Cooperative — Schedule of Proposed Rate Changes (2026-08-08)","url":"https://avec.org/2026/08/08/schedule-of-proposed-rate-changes/","quote":"Energy Charge (0-750 kWh) $0.3647/kWh","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Alaska Village Electric Cooperative — The Cost of Fuel & Need for Greater PCE Funding (2026-04-01)","url":"https://avec.org/2026/04/01/the-cost-of-fuel-need-for-greater-pce-funding/","quote":"AVEC submits a filing with the Regulatory Commission of Alaska (RCA) to update its Cost of Power Adjustment (COPA)","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"labrador-northern-ontario","region":"Labrador and remote northern Ontario First Nations","country":"CA","jurisdiction":"Canadian province (north)","power_system":"Nunatsiavut's five communities (Nain, Hopedale, Postville, Makkovik, Rigolet) are each an isolated diesel microgrid operated by Newfoundland and Labrador Hydro, unconnected to the provincial grid and resupplied by ship in the ice-free season; Nain's own generating station is listed at 3.86 MW (diesel genset, commissioned 1974). In northwestern Ontario, the Wataynikaneyap ('Watay') Power transmission project, majority-owned by 24 First Nations with Fortis Inc., built roughly 1,800 km of line at a Canada-government commitment of $1.6 billion to connect remote diesel communities to Ontario's grid; the first community (Pikangikum) was energized in December 2018, with the project since expanding toward the 24 partner communities.","price":"No published $/kWh figure fetched this pass for Nunatsiavut communities","nuclear_position":"No Nunatsiavut, NunatuKavut, Innu Nation or Watay-partner nuclear position found; Newfoundland and Labrador's provincial energy policy materials reviewed carry no nuclear content.","microreactor_activity":"None found. NL Hydro's own renewables page for isolated systems lists only solar installations (completed in all Nunatsiavut communities) and a preliminary wind/battery system-impact study for Nain, no nuclear content.","microreactor_read":"Neither sub-region shows any reactor interest; Labrador's loads (sub-4 MW per community) are too small individually, and northern Ontario's diesel communities are being solved by grid extension (Watay), not generation, leaving no addressable microreactor buyer identified here.","group":"canada-north","position":"silent","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[{"name":"Nain generating station (NL Hydro)","mw":"3.86 MW","note":"diesel genset, commissioned 1974"},{"name":"Watay Power service territory","mw":"~1,800 km of transmission line, $1.6 billion federal commitment","note":"connects up to 24 First Nations formerly on diesel"}],"blockers":["No community identified draws anywhere near a microreactor-scale load (Nain's 3.86 MW station is representative of the region's scale)","Watay Power's grid-extension model (diesel-to-transmission-interconnection) is the incumbent solution actively being built out, reducing the addressable off-grid population","No nuclear-specific policy, study or vendor engagement was found anywhere in this region"],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia — List of generating stations in Newfoundland and Labrador","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_generating_stations_in_Newfoundland_and_Labrador","quote":"( Nain ) 3.86 1974 Newfoundland and Labrador Hydro Diesel genset","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Wataynikaneyap Power — Wataynikaneyap Power LP and Government of Canada Formalize Support for Province's Largest First Nations-led Transmission Project","url":"https://www.wataypower.ca/updates/wataynikaneyap-power-lp-and-government-of-canada-formalize-support-for-provinces-largest-first-nations-led-transmission-project","quote":"Approximately 1,800 kilometres of transmission lines in Northwestern Ontario","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"northwest-territories","region":"Northwest Territories","country":"CA","jurisdiction":"Canadian territory","power_system":"The Northwest Territories 'does not have a territorial electricity grid'; eight communities around Great Slave Lake share two regional hydro grids, and of the remaining 25 communities, 23 rely on diesel-fired power plants. Canada's energy regulator puts NWT's 'estimated generating capacity' at 233 MW total, of which diesel plants 'accounted for 68%... in 2021.' The territorial utility, NTPC, operates diesel plants ranging from under 0.1 MW (Buffalo Junction) to 27.66 MW (a diesel combustion turbine at Yellowknife).","price":"No consumer $/kWh figure fetched this pass; NTPC/NWT Power Corporation rates are set by the territorial Public Utilities Board","nuclear_position":"No territorial ban or enabling law found; the NWT was not identified in Canada's SMR Roadmap materials as having taken a specific policy position, unlike Yukon's April 2026 Ontario partnership.","microreactor_activity":"None found specific to the NWT government or NTPC. The territory appears only as photographed context ('A power plant in the Northwest Territories') in the federal Department of National Defence's August 2026 Arctic microreactor feasibility story (see the nunavut record), not as a named host.","microreactor_read":"NWT's diamond mines (Ekati at 30.8 MW, Diavik at 55.4 MW) are exactly the scale a relocatable reactor targets, but Diavik just ended mining (March 2026) and Ekati's remaining mine life was not established this pass; NWT lags Yukon in any government-level nuclear engagement.","group":"canada-north","position":"silent","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[{"name":"Ekati diamond mine","mw":"30.8 MW installed (seven 4.4 MW diesel generators)","note":"off-grid"},{"name":"Diavik diamond mine (Rio Tinto)","mw":"55.4 MW wind-diesel hybrid facility","note":"off-grid; last ore milestone was March 2026 and the mine is 'progressing through decommissioning and reclamation'"}],"blockers":["No territorial SMR policy, RFI or utility study was found, unlike Yukon","Diavik, the largest single diesel-hybrid load identified, ended ore production in March 2026 and is now in decommissioning, removing it as a live reactor prospect","23 of NWT's diesel communities are individually small (sub-3 MW in most cases per NTPC's own community list), similar to rural Alaska's PCE villages"],"sources":[{"label":"Canada Energy Regulator — Provincial and Territorial Energy Profiles: Northwest Territories","url":"https://www.cer-rec.gc.ca/en/data-analysis/energy-markets/province-territory-energy-profiles/northwest-territories.html","quote":"NWT has an estimated generating capacity of 233 megawatts (MW).","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Rio Tinto — Diavik, Canada (operations page)","url":"https://www.riotinto.com/en/operations/canada/diavik","quote":"Diavik Diamond Mine's last ore milestone took place in March 2026","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"nunavik-northern-quebec","region":"Nunavik (northern Quebec)","country":"CA","jurisdiction":"Canadian province (north)","power_system":"Hydro-Quebec Remote Communities serves 14 Nunavik villages, each 'a power plant... located in your village [that] uses diesel fuel to generate the electricity needed in your home', off-grid, no connection to Hydro-Quebec's main hydro system despite the utility being one of the world's largest hydro generators. Raglan mine (Glencore), Nunavik's largest industrial load, runs 'an off-grid power system consisting of over 20 generators that consume several thousand litres of diesel fuel per hour.'","price":"No published $/kWh figure fetched this pass for Nunavik village tariffs","nuclear_position":"No Nunavik-specific nuclear policy found. At the provincial level, Hydro-Quebec's only nuclear plant, Gentilly-2, closed in 2012; press reporting (not independently fetched this pass) indicates the utility studied recommissioning it in 2023 and chose not to proceed. Quebec's Strategic Plan 2022-2026 frames its clean-electricity growth (100+ TWh needed for carbon neutrality by 2050) around hydro expansion, not nuclear, consistent with no Nunavik nuclear activity being found.","microreactor_activity":"None found. All identified Nunavik decarbonization activity (Quaqtaq/Puvirnituq wind, Raglan's TUGLIQ turbines) is wind- or solar-based, run by Hydro-Quebec or TUGLIQ, with no reactor study identified.","microreactor_read":"Nunavik's off-grid diesel villages and Raglan mine are physically the right scale, but Quebec's provincial nuclear posture (an abandoned Gentilly-2 restart) and Hydro-Quebec's active wind/solar conversion program both point away from nuclear as the near-term answer here.","group":"canada-north","position":"silent","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[{"name":"Raglan mine (Glencore)","mw":"20+ diesel generators, several thousand litres/hour","note":"TUGLIQ Energy's 2 wind turbines (since 2014) save an estimated 4.4 million litres of diesel/year"},{"name":"Quaqtaq and Puvirnituq (wind project)","mw":"not disclosed","note":"Hydro-Quebec states the project would meet roughly 60% of the two villages' needs and save 96 million litres of diesel over 25 years (snippet-only, source access blocked)"}],"blockers":["Quebec's provincial nuclear posture is now inactive by its own choice (Gentilly-2 recommissioning study abandoned on social-acceptability grounds)","Hydro-Quebec's Nunavik strategy is wind/solar/battery conversion of its 14 diesel villages, already funded and underway, reducing near-term appetite for a new technology","Raglan already has an operating wind-diesel hybrid (TUGLIQ, since 2014) that is the incumbent 'what a reactor has to beat' precedent"],"sources":[{"label":"Hydro-Quebec — Energy efficiency in Nunavik","url":"https://www.hydroquebec.com/energy-efficiency-nunavik/","quote":"The power plant is located in your village. It uses diesel fuel to generate the electricity needed in your home.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"World Nuclear News — Hydro-Quebec mulls Gentilly 2 recommissioning","url":"https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Hydro-Quebec-mulls-Gentilly-2-recommissioning","quote":"Hydro-Québec mulls Gentilly 2 recommissioning","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"nunavut","region":"Nunavut","country":"CA","jurisdiction":"Canadian territory","power_system":"Qulliq Energy Corporation (QEC) runs 25 stand-alone diesel plants (~76 MW total, 19 over 1 MW, Iqaluit largest at 13.6 MW, established in the 2026-09-02 pass and not re-verified here). New this pass: the Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link (Nukik Corporation, Inuit-owned) proposes a 1,200 km, 150 MW transmission and fibre line from Manitoba's grid to five Kivalliq communities and at least one gold mine.","price":"Interim 2025 QEC rates of $555-1,130/MWh by class (established in the 2026-09-02 pass)","nuclear_position":"No territorial nuclear ban or enabling law found. QEC's own 2023-2026 Corporate Plan does not mention nuclear power (established in the 2026-09-02 pass). New: the federal Department of National Defence confirmed in August 2026 that it 'has launched a multi-year program to see whether a small nuclear reactor can be deployed in the Arctic,' funding the defence company Canadian Strategic Missions Corporation (CSMC) with $4.5 million in July 2026 'to advance Canadian-made energy solutions that will secure the North.'","microreactor_activity":"Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link schedule/funding, updated this pass: the Government of Canada's most recent dedicated funding announcement (March 2024) added $2.8 million to the project's development phase (on top of $11.6 million already provided for planning/feasibility), stating 'construction is expected to begin by 2028.' Since then, Nukik Corporation reports filing a Transmission Service Request with Manitoba Hydro (by December 2025) and becoming 'the first Arctic electricity developer in North America to be certified by the Midcontinent Independent System Operator (MISO)'; on July 7, 2026 the governments of Nunavut and Manitoba signed an MOU reaffirming 'Manitoba's commitment to provide 50 megawatts of firm electric capacity to the project.' No newer construction-start or financial-close date than 2028 was found. Separately, on the federal nuclear-for-the-North question: CSMC's CEO Daniel Sax said the company is 'working on building... very small nuclear reactors deployable into the Arctic to replace the diesel generators that are up there,' with 'a demonstration of the nuclear reactor by 2028 and then... this technology into the field by 2030,' and 'it's still unclear where in the North it will go.' Canadian Nuclear Laboratories' Ryan Clarke separately noted the North is 'already in an energy deficit.'","microreactor_read":"Nunavut now has two parallel federal tracks, a 150 MW transmission alternative (Kivalliq, construction not before 2028) and a dedicated federal microreactor program (demonstration by 2028, field deployment by 2030), neither of which has picked a site; QEC's own interim rates ($555-1,130/MWh) remain the strongest economic case in this entire file for a reactor to beat.","group":"canada-north","position":"silent","price_short":"$555-1,130/MWh (QEC, 2025)","loads":[{"name":"Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link (proposed)","mw":"150 MW, 1,200 km transmission line","note":"would take 5 communities (Arviat, Baker Lake, Chesterfield Inlet, Rankin Inlet, Whale Cove) and at least one gold mine off diesel; construction expected to begin by 2028"}],"blockers":["Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link construction is not expected to begin before 2028, and no financial close date was found","The federal microreactor-for-the-Arctic program is explicitly pre-siting: 'it's still unclear where in the North it will go,' with a 2028 demonstration and 2030 field target","Permafrost-melt risk from reactor waste heat and Arctic nuclear-waste storage were both raised as open questions by Canadian Nuclear Laboratories in the same August 2026 reporting"],"sources":[{"label":"Government of Canada (Crown-Indigenous Relations and Northern Affairs) — Minister Vandal announces federal funding towards Kivalliq Hydro-Fibre Link (2024-03-21)","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/crown-indigenous-relations-northern-affairs/news/2024/03/minister-vandal-announces-federal-funding-towards-kivalliq-hydro-fibre-link-to-advance-clean-energy-transition-and-broadband-service-delivery-to-th.html","quote":"Construction is expected to begin by 2028.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"GlobeNewswire (Nukik Corporation release) — Nukik Corporation Welcomes the MOU Signing Between the Governments of Nunavut and Manitoba (2026-07-08)","url":"https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/07/08/3324346/0/en/Nukik-Corporation-Welcomes-the-MOU-Signing-Between-the-Governments-of-Nunavut-and-Manitoba.html","quote":"Manitoba's commitment to provide 50 megawatts of firm electric capacity to the project","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Radio Canada International / Eye on the Arctic (CBC News) — To help solve the North's 'energy deficit', Ottawa looks at nuclear options (2026-08-06)","url":"https://www.rcinet.ca/eye-on-the-arctic/2026/08/06/to-help-solve-the-norths-energy-deficit-ottawa-looks-at-nuclear-options/","quote":"very small nuclear reactors deployable into the Arctic to replace the diesel generators that are up there","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"yukon","region":"Yukon","country":"CA","jurisdiction":"Canadian territory","power_system":"Yukon Energy runs a predominantly hydro grid (Whitehorse/Aishihik/Mayo) backstopped by LNG and diesel for winter peaking; low water in the Aishihik reservoir constrained winter 2025-26 output, and Yukon Energy is building a 15 MW thermal power centre in South Whitehorse using relocated rental diesel units. LNG plants held 13.2 MW (8.7%) of 2024 installed capacity per Yukon government statistics.","price":"No consumer $/kWh figure fetched this pass; see Yukon Energy's own rate filings with the Yukon Utilities Board for tariffs","nuclear_position":"No territorial ban. On April 22, 2026, the Government of Yukon signed a formal partnership agreement with the Government of Ontario 'to strengthen collaboration on energy development, with a focus on improving long-term reliability and exploring emerging technologies such as small modular reactors.'","microreactor_activity":"Under the April 2026 Yukon-Ontario agreement, 'Ontario Power Generation and Yukon Energy will collaborate on the necessary groundwork to deploy SMRs to Yukon's grid,' covering 'nuclear energy, SMRs and microreactors including technological readiness, regulatory frameworks, supply chains, economics and financing, nuclear waste management and public and Indigenous engagement.' Yukon's energy minister, Ted Laking, framed it as a direct response to winter reliability: 'Our electricity grid is under strain and will soon be unable to meet the needs of the territory... we require significant investment in new generation capacity we can rely on in our coldest and darkest months of the year.' This is an assessment/knowledge-sharing agreement, not a project commitment, contract or site selection.","microreactor_read":"Yukon is the only Canadian territory with a named, dated, government-to-government nuclear partnership (April 2026) driven explicitly by a winter capacity shortfall; it is an assessment stage, but it is further along the policy path than Nunavut or NWT.","group":"canada-north","position":"study","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[{"name":"Eagle Gold mine (Victoria Gold)","mw":"grid-connected via a 45 km, 69kV powerline; diesel emergency backup only","note":"not off-grid despite remote location"},{"name":"Minto mine (Capstone Mining, now closed)","mw":"grid-connected","note":"grid connection over self-generation saved an estimated 53,000 tonnes/year of GHG emissions vs. diesel"}],"blockers":["The agreement is explicitly exploratory ('help inform Yukon's assessment of whether SMRs could play a role'), with no project, site or vendor named","No Yukon-specific nuclear siting or licensing framework exists yet; the territory would rely on federal CNSC licensing and whatever regulatory groundwork the Ontario partnership produces","Yukon Energy's near-term capital is committed to the 15 MW South Whitehorse thermal centre and existing hydro assets"],"sources":[{"label":"Government of Yukon — Yukon and Ontario sign partnership agreement to explore energy solutions, including small modular reactors (2026-04-23, news release #26-111)","url":"https://yukon.ca/en/news/yukon-and-ontario-sign-partnership-agreement-explore-energy-solutions-including-small-modular-reactors","quote":"Ontario Power Generation and Yukon Energy will collaborate on the necessary groundwork to deploy SMRs to Yukon's grid","status":"fetched"},{"label":"NS Energy Business / Global Energy Monitor-style project page — Eagle Gold Mine, Mayo Mining District, Yukon","url":"https://www.nsenergybusiness.com/projects/eagle-gold-mine/","quote":"Power supply for the project will be provided by a 45km-long, 69kV powerline from the Yukon electrical grid.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"greenland","region":"Greenland","country":"GL","jurisdiction":"Danish autonomous territory","power_system":"Nukissiorfiit, Greenland's sole state-owned utility, supplies electricity, water and (in some places) heat to 'approximately 56,000 residents across 17 towns and 54 settlements' with 'no nationwide or regional electricity grid', every town and settlement is its own isolated system. Hydropower stations exist only at Nuuk, Sisimiut, Ilulissat and Tasiilaq; 'the power stations in all other towns and settlements are operated with diesel generators.' Nukissiorfiit reports about 72% of its energy from renewables (mostly hydro), targeting fossil-free supply by 2030.","price":"No DKK/kWh tariff sheet found (Nukissiorfiit's price pages 404'd); Nukissiorfiit states it subsidizes rates uniformly across Greenland to offset the gap between cheap hydro towns and small diesel-only settlements","nuclear_position":"Greenland's own Uranium Act (Greenland Parliament Act No. 20) 'came into force' on 2 December 2021 and bans 'prospecting, exploration, and exploitation of uranium' as well as any other mineral resource whose 'average uranium content' exceeds '100 ppm by weight.' On 26 June 2026 Greenland's government rejected Energy Transition Minerals' licence-renewal application for the Kvanefjeld/Kuannersuit deposit, stating 'further exploration in the area is not deemed likely to lead to the discovery of deposits that can be exploited in accordance with the Uranium Act.' Denmark, which controls defense and largely leaves mineral/energy policy to Greenland's home rule but retains the 1985 nationwide nuclear-power ban's legal legacy, announced in January 2026 that it is reviewing that 40-year ban: 'Denmark is considering building small modular nuclear reactors to meet its energy needs... which would bring an end to its 1985 ban on nuclear power,' after the Danish parliament 'agreed in early 2025 to begin examining new nuclear power technologies.' No specific Greenland carve-out or position within that Danish review was found.","microreactor_activity":"None found. The Danish SMR review is national and does not name Greenland; Nukissiorfiit has made no public statement on nuclear power.","microreactor_read":"Greenland is the least-prepared civilian buyer in this file: no enabling law, no utility study, and its parent state is only beginning to reconsider a 40-year nuclear ban. A reactor sale here depends on Denmark's still-open 2026 SMR review landing somewhere favorable, which has not yet happened.","group":"arctic-europe","position":"review","price_short":"tariff not published","loads":[{"name":"Nukissiorfiit system-wide","mw":"17 towns, 54 settlements, ~56,000 residents","note":"no interconnecting grid; hydro at 4 towns only, diesel everywhere else"},{"name":"Tanbreez rare earth project (Critical Metals Corp, 70% acquired)","mw":"n/a (pre-production)","note":"site sits 2 km from an existing hydro power line; targeting Q4 2028/Q1 2029 production"},{"name":"Kvanefjeld/Kuannersuit rare earth-uranium deposit (Energy Transition Minerals)","mw":"n/a (licence renewal rejected 2026-06-26)","note":"uranium content ~300 ppm, exceeds the Uranium Act's 100 ppm threshold"}],"blockers":["The Uranium Act bans uranium mining/byproducts above 100 ppm, and Greenland's government has just (2026) shown it will enforce that against a major rare-earth project (Kvanefjeld)","Denmark's own nuclear review is at an early, information-gathering stage with no timeline for a political decision","No town or settlement publishes a load anywhere near even the smallest current microreactor design; the entire population (~56,000) is spread across 71 separate systems"],"sources":[{"label":"Nukissiorfiit — Home (English)","url":"https://nukissiorfiit.gl/en/","quote":"Nukissiorfiit's supply obligation covers 17 towns and 54 settlements.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Greenland Mineral Resources Authority (Naalakkersuisut) — The ban on uranium (2022)","url":"https://govmin.gl/2022/04/the-ban-on-uranium/","quote":"Greenland Parliament Act No. 20 of 1 December 2021 to ban uranium prospecting, exploration and exploitation, etc. (the Uranium Act) came into force","status":"fetched"},{"label":"MINING.COM (Reuters) — Greenland rejects Energy Transition Minerals' request for licence renewal (2026-06-26)","url":"https://www.mining.com/web/greenland-rejects-energy-transition-minerals-request-for-licence-renewal/","quote":"Further exploration in the area is not deemed likely to lead to the discovery of deposits that can be exploited in accordance with the Uranium Act","status":"fetched"},{"label":"The Local Denmark (AFP) — Denmark mulls end of 1985 nuclear power ban (2026-01-22)","url":"https://www.thelocal.dk/20260122/denmark-mulls-end-of-1985-nuclear-power-ban","quote":"Denmark is considering building small modular nuclear reactors to meet its energy needs","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"polar-research-stations","region":"Polar research stations (Antarctica and Greenland ice sheet)","country":"AQ/GL","jurisdiction":"Antarctic Treaty System / Danish autonomous territory (research stations, not a national jurisdiction)","power_system":"The only civilian nuclear power precedent at either pole is the US Navy's PM-3A reactor at McMurdo Station, Antarctica: a 'pressurised-water reactor, with a capacity of 1.8 megawatts' by one account and '1,500 kilowatt' by another, which 'operated from March 1962 - September 1972' and displaced 'the need to deliver millions of gallons of fuel oil.' Today, US Antarctic and Greenland-ice-sheet stations (e.g. Summit Station, Greenland, run on behalf of the National Science Foundation at 3,200 m elevation) are diesel-generator powered, resupplied by aircraft (LC-130 airlift) given the absence of any road or port access.","price":"No $/gallon or $/MWh fuel-logistics figure was found for Summit Station specifically; Greenland's general retail diesel price was $2.42/gallon as of 16 April 2026 (national average, not station-specific)","nuclear_position":"No nuclear ban specific to research stations; the Antarctic Treaty System restricts nuclear explosions and radioactive waste disposal in Antarctica but has historically permitted power reactors (PM-3A operated under it). No current proposal to re-introduce nuclear power at any US, Greenland or Antarctic research station was found.","microreactor_activity":"None found for any current station. PM-3A remains a one-off historical case, shut down for cause (a water leak judged 'impractical to repair,' compounded by the cost of its required 25-person crew and 'concerns over possible chloride stress corrosion').","microreactor_read":"The historical McMurdo PM-3A case is the only civilian precedent for polar reactor power anywhere in this file, and it argues both ways: it proved a reactor can displace millions of gallons of resupply fuel, but its 1972 shutdown for a maintenance failure and its need for a 25-person crew are exactly the reliability and staffing risks a modern remote microreactor pitch has to answer.","group":"arctic-europe","position":"silent","price_short":"diesel $2.42/gal (Greenland retail)","loads":[{"name":"McMurdo Station PM-3A (historical, 1962-1972)","mw":"1.5 MW (1,500 kW)","note":"shut down after a 1972 water leak; decommissioning completed 1979"},{"name":"Summit Station, Greenland (NSF)","mw":"diesel generator, capacity not published","note":"3,200 m elevation; airlift-resupplied from Kangerlussuaq"}],"blockers":["PM-3A's own history is a cautionary precedent, not an endorsement: it was down more than a quarter of the time and required an expensive dedicated crew before being decommissioned","No current station operator (NSF, the US Antarctic Program) has an active nuclear proposal","Fuel-logistics cost data specific to any single station (e.g. Summit) is not published in a form this pass could locate or fetch"],"sources":[{"label":"Atomic Insights — McMurdo Station: the New York of the Deep Freeze South","url":"https://atomicinsights.com/mcmurdo-station-new-york-deep-freeze-south/","quote":"the 1,500 kilowatt power station (nicknamed Nukey-Poo) was providing electricity and heat","status":"fetched"},{"label":"The Conversation — Remembering Antarctica's nuclear past with 'Nukey Poo'","url":"https://theconversation.com/remembering-antarcticas-nuclear-past-with-nukey-poo-99934","quote":"The 25-man team required to run the plant was expensive","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"svalbard-and-northern-nordics","region":"Svalbard and northern Norway/Finland/Sweden","country":"NO","jurisdiction":"Norwegian territory","power_system":"Longyearbyen (Svalbard) closed the world's northernmost coal power plant on 19 October 2023 and switched to a temporary diesel plant while it builds toward an all-renewable supply; the coal-to-diesel transition itself was budgeted at 'NOK 212 million,' with the associated Mine 7 coal mine closing by summer 2025. In mainland northern Norway, Norsk Kjernekraft has signed cooperation/study agreements covering at least nine municipalities nationwide, including the northern municipalities of Narvik and Vardo; Narvik's municipal development company Futurum has formed 'Narvik Kjernekraft AS' with Norsk Kjernekraft to investigate SMR-based power plants. In Finland, Steady Energy (a VTT spinout) is marketing its LDR-50 reactor, rated at 'maximum heating power 50 MW per reactor module' for district heating, desalination and industrial steam, with letters of intent from the cities of Kuopio, Helsinki, Jyvaskyla (Alva) and, most recently, Kerava (Keravan Energia).","price":"No $/kWh or $/MWh figure fetched this pass for Longyearbyen's post-coal diesel supply or any Nordic SMR-district-heat customer","nuclear_position":"Norway has no nationwide nuclear-power ban and several municipalities (including the northern towns of Narvik and Vardo) have signed exploratory agreements with Norsk Kjernekraft. Finland permits nuclear power and already operates large reactors; Steady Energy's LDR-50 district-heat design is pursuing city-level letters of intent rather than a national policy change. Svalbard's own governance (the Norwegian state, via the Svalbard Treaty framework) has made no nuclear statement; its post-coal plan is diesel-then-renewables, not nuclear.","microreactor_activity":"Narvik: 'Narvik Kjernekraft AS' formed with Norsk Kjernekraft to begin 'planning a formal assessment phase' for possible SMR construction, exploratory, no reactor size or in-service date set. Steady Energy: LDR-50 letters of intent signed with multiple Finnish cities, still at feasibility/permitting-scoping stage, with a company target of a first LDR-50 plant 'by 2030.' No microreactor activity found at Longyearbyen itself.","microreactor_read":"Northern Norway (Narvik, Vardo) and Finland's district-heat cities are the most advanced Nordic microreactor prospects in named-agreement terms, but all remain pre-feasibility; Svalbard itself, despite being the most extreme and best-known cold, remote, post-coal town in the region, has no reactor proposal at all.","group":"arctic-europe","position":"study","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[{"name":"Longyearbyen townsite (Svalbard)","mw":"diesel interim supply since Oct 2023","note":"coal plant closed 2023-10-19; renewable target 2030"},{"name":"Narvik / Vardo (northern Norway)","mw":"SMR feasibility study stage only","note":"Norsk Kjernekraft municipal agreement, no MW or site chosen"},{"name":"Kuopio/Helsinki/Jyvaskyla/Kerava district heat (Finland)","mw":"50 MW thermal per LDR-50 module","note":"letters of intent / pre-investment studies, not contracted"}],"blockers":["Longyearbyen's own plan is renewables by 2030, not nuclear; no SMR proposal was found for Svalbard specifically","Narvik/Vardo agreements are pre-feasibility ('investigate, and possibly plan, build and operate') with no site, size or schedule set","Steady Energy's LDR-50 has signed customer interest but no LDR-50 unit has yet been built anywhere"],"sources":[{"label":"High North News — All Ready for the Transition From Coal to Diesel in Longyearbyen","url":"https://en.highnorthnews.com/politics/all-ready-for-the-transition-from-coal-to-diesel-in-longyearbyen/134303","quote":"The transition to diesel is, according to Svalbard Energy, expected to cost NOK 212 million.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Steady Energy — LDR-50 (solution page)","url":"https://www.steadyenergy.com/ldr-50","quote":"Maximum heating power 50 MW per reactor module","status":"fetched"},{"label":"NucNet — Narvik Kjernekraft AS formation with Norsk Kjernekraft (via search reporting)","url":"https://www.nucnet.org/news/nuclear-company-submits-proposal-for-smr-station-in-north-of-country-6-1-2024","quote":"Nuclear Company Submits Proposal For SMR Station In North Of Country","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"american-samoa","region":"American Samoa","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US territory","power_system":"American Samoa Power Authority (ASPA) generates almost entirely on imported diesel, diesel-dependent generation is about 97% of output, with solar about 11% of installed utility-scale capacity but only about 3% of generation (2023), and 'spends about $27 million to $30 million on diesel every year.' Construction began in 2024 on the first phase of a 20-megawatt Pavaiai solar PV project, and a 4-megawatt/8-megawatt-hour battery system was commissioned in early 2025.","price":"43 cents/kWh average retail price, 2023 (about 3.5x the US average); ASPA states 'ASPA's cost for electric generation today is about 27-cents per kilowatt hour' (24 cents fuel, 3 cents O&M), projected to fall to about 11 cents/kWh once new solar and wind independent-power-producer contracts are online","nuclear_position":"No nuclear ban or enabling law found; no statement located from ASPA, the territorial government or the Fono on nuclear power or microreactors in this search pass.","microreactor_activity":"none found, searched ASPA's own site and general microreactor literature for any American-Samoa-specific MOU, feasibility study or vendor visit. Any future outreach should be handled with care given the wider Pacific region's history with nuclear-weapons-test fallout, even though American Samoa itself was not a test site.","microreactor_read":"ASPA's diesel bill (about 27 cents/kWh, $27-30M/year in fuel alone) is exactly the delivered-fuel cost a reactor should beat, and Tutuila's 27 MW yearly system peak is inside the 1-20 MW range several times over, but ASPA has already committed its near-term capital to solar-plus-storage (Pavaiai, and previously Ta'u) rather than nuclear, and no study has opened that door.","group":"us-islands","position":"silent","price_short":"$0.43/kWh average (2023)","loads":[{"name":"ASPA Tutuila system","mw":"27 MW yearly peak","note":"system peak with about 35 MW of units on line; ASPA's bid documents name a StarKist feeder but publish no cannery load"},{"name":"Ta'u island microgrid","mw":"1.41 MW solar + 6 MWh battery (60 Tesla Powerpacks)","note":"off-grid; converted from 100% diesel to about 100% solar, completed November 2016"}],"blockers":["No nuclear feasibility study, MOU or legislative interest found to build on","ASPA's capital program is already committed to the Pavaiai solar (20 MW) and battery (4 MW/8 MWh) builds now under construction","The StarKist cannery feeder (27 MW peak) is close to the top of the 1-20 MW range alone, on a small isolated grid with limited other load to spread fixed reactor costs across"],"sources":[{"label":"Samoa News — ASPA rates to drop significantly when two energy projects completed","url":"https://www.samoanews.com/local-news/aspa-rates-drop-significantly-when-two-energy-projects-completed","quote":"ASPA's cost for electric generation today is about 27-cents per kilowatt hour","status":"fetched"},{"label":"National Geographic — Ta'u, American Samoa, Changed From Diesel to Solar Power With Tesla's SolarCity","url":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/tau-american-samoa-solar-power-microgrid-tesla-solarcity","quote":"The $8 million project was funded by the U.S. Department of Interior and the American Samoa Power Authority (ASPA).","status":"fetched"},{"label":"American Samoa Power Authority — Emergency Bids Addendum 9 (feeder load schedule)","url":"https://www.aspower.com/aspaweb/emergencybids/Addendum9.pdf","quote":"with the yearly peak of 27 mW","status":"fetched"},{"label":"U.S. Energy Information Administration — American Samoa territory profile","url":"https://www.eia.gov/state/?sid=AQ","quote":"diesel-dependent generation still comprises 97% of ASPA's output","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"guam","region":"Guam","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US territory","power_system":"Guam Power Authority (GPA) is the sole civilian utility; its oil-fired fleet has been joined by the new Ukudu power plant, which a Consolidated Commission on Utilities member said in November 2024 'can make power at 5 (cents) per kWh plus fuel at 11 cents to 13 cents per kWh', roughly 16-18 cents/kWh all-in, cheaper than the legacy oil-fired units it supplements.","price":"LEAC (fuel cost adjustment) proposed to fall from $0.208802/kWh to $0.188781/kWh effective 2025-08-01, then to $0.135840/kWh effective 2025-09-01 as Ukudu came online; the residential base rate was separately proposed to rise about 2.2-2.8 cents/kWh in the same filing","nuclear_position":"GPA operates no licensed reactor. A 2023 territorial bill, the 'Nuclear Energy Prohibition Act' (Bill 151-37, introduced by Sen. Sabina Flores Perez, Speaker Therese Terlaje and Sen. Chris Barnett), would add a new Guam Code chapter under which 'no nuclear fission power plant, small modular reactors or nuclear microreactors shall be constructed, imported or used, and no radioactive material shall be disposed of on Guam'; no enactment into public law was confirmed in this pass, and as of Nov 2024-Feb 2025 reporting it remained a live proposal under committee discussion. Guam's own Public Utilities Commission chairman argued against it, saying 'the Legislature should not prohibit fair and open considerations of all technologies.'","microreactor_activity":"Secondary reporting describes a 2012 GPA dialogue with NuScale Power on small modular reactors, not independently confirmed against a NuScale or GPA primary source in this pass. In November 2024 a Consolidated Commission on Utilities member cited small (1-3 MW) nuclear plant power costs of 47 cents/kWh against Ukudu's 16-18 cents/kWh all-in, concluding 'someday, nuclear can make sense. But not in the near future.'","microreactor_read":"GPA's newest conventional plant already prices near 16-18 cents/kWh and the territory's own utility regulator cites 47 cents/kWh for small nuclear, so civilian Guam is not a near-term buyer until unit cost falls well below an already-improving oil-fired benchmark, and a pending territorial ban would have to be resolved first.","group":"us-islands","position":"proposed-ban","price_short":"fuel charge $0.136/kWh (2025)","loads":[],"blockers":["A pending territorial bill would prohibit constructing, importing or using any nuclear fission plant, SMR or microreactor on Guam; final legislative status unconfirmed in this pass","GPA's own utility commissioner puts small-reactor power cost (47 cents/kWh) at roughly 3x the new Ukudu gas/diesel plant's all-in cost (16-18 cents/kWh)","Separate 2026-2027 NDAA activity concerns a Department of Defense SMR pilot for Indo-Pacific Command, a defense track distinct from, and politically entangled with, GPA's civilian system"],"sources":[{"label":"Pacific Island Times — Bill would ban nuclear energy on Guam (2023)","url":"https://www.pacificislandtimes.com/post/bill-would-ban-nuclear-energy-on-guam","quote":"No nuclear fission power plant, small modular reactors or nuclear microreactors shall be constructed, imported or used, and no radioactive material shall be disposed of on Guam","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Post Guam — Sanchez: Nuclear power could make sense for Guam someday, but not anytime soon (2024-11-23, updated 2025-02-11)","url":"https://www.postguam.com/news/local/sanchez-nuclear-power-could-make-sense-for-guam-someday-but-not-anytime-soon/article_17f26662-a6ea-11ef-ae97-6b086eba03a3.html","quote":"Ukudu can make power at 5 (cents) per kWh plus fuel at 11 cents to 13 cents per kWh","status":"fetched"},{"label":"KANDIT News Group — Guam Power Rates Likely to Go Down Again, and Sooner Than Planned (2025)","url":"https://kanditnews.com/guam-power-rates-likely-to-go-down-again-and-sooner-than-planned/","quote":"proposed LEAC rate reduction to $0.135840/kWh effective September 1, 2025","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"hawaii","region":"Hawaii","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US state","power_system":"Hawaiian Electric (HECO, plus its Hawai'i Electric Light and Maui Electric subsidiaries) and the member-owned Kaua'i Island Utility Cooperative (KIUC) run separate, non-interconnected island grids. HECO's companies still burn mostly oil: fuel-mix filings put Hawai'i Electric Light (Hawai'i Island) at roughly 35% HSFO / 65% diesel and Maui Electric (serving Maui, Molokai and Lanai) at roughly 24% HSFO / 76% diesel, and HECO said in March 2026 it had raised renewables to 37% of generation across its islands, targeting 40% by 2030. KIUC reports about 60% of its Kaua'i generation from renewables.","price":"39.62 cents/kWh statewide average, March 2025, 'well over double the national average'; KIUC's Kaua'i rate ran 33-39 cents/kWh (2025). A 2026 oil-price spike (crude near $90/barrel in July 2026 versus $65-70/barrel a year earlier) drove June-2025-to-June-2026 bill increases of 27% on Maui, 30% on Oahu and 55% on Lanai.","nuclear_position":"Hawaii's state constitution, Article XI Section 8 (added by a 1978 amendment), provides that 'No nuclear fission power plant shall be constructed or radioactive material disposed of in the State without the prior approval by a two-thirds vote in each house of the legislature.' Rep. Elijah Pierick's HB1741 (2024) proposed repealing that provision but 'did not receive a hearing.' In February 2025 the legislature instead advanced Senate Bill 1588 (Energy and Intergovernmental Affairs Committee 5-0, Ways and Means 13-0) as Senate Concurrent Resolution 136, creating a nuclear energy task force.","microreactor_activity":"The SCR136 working group's report to the legislature, 'Advanced Nuclear Power Technologies in Hawai'i' (Hawaii State Energy Office, December 2025), examined both 1-20 MW microreactors and 20-300 MW SMRs and 'ultimately recommended against any advanced nuclear development in the state,' on the grounds that the technologies 'are neither commercially viable nor technically proven' and that Hawaii 'currently lacks the facilities, workforce expertise, regulatory framework, and emergency-response capabilities to handle, process, and store radioactive waste.' It recommended the state 'continue to monitor global advancements and revisit this assessment periodically.' A separate August 2026 Hawaii media piece was found referencing public debate over a proposed Radiant 'Kaleidos' microreactor in the state, but this pass could not fetch that article (connection error) to confirm site, proposer or MW.","microreactor_read":"Hawaii pays close to the highest delivered-fuel electricity price in the US, spiking further on 2026 oil prices, and neighbor-island grids like Lanai and Molokai are small and diesel-heavy, exactly the profile a microreactor pitch wants, but the state's own December 2025 study reviewed the 1-20 MW class specifically and recommended against development on top of a constitutional two-thirds-vote requirement, making this a policy-gated market until that report's premises change.","group":"us-islands","position":"restricted","price_short":"$0.396/kWh average (2025)","loads":[{"name":"Molokai and Lanai (Maui Electric-served neighbor islands)","mw":"part of Maui Electric's roughly 24% HSFO / 76% diesel system","note":"small, diesel-heavy island grids; Lanai's bill rose 55% June 2025-June 2026 on oil prices"}],"blockers":["State constitution requires a two-thirds vote in each legislative house before any nuclear fission plant can be built or waste disposed of in the state","The state's own December 2025 SCR136 working-group report recommended against any advanced nuclear development, citing commercial/technical immaturity and the state's lack of radioactive-waste infrastructure","Neither HECO/its subsidiaries nor KIUC has taken a public position in favor of a specific reactor project"],"sources":[{"label":"American Nuclear Society Nuclear Newswire — Hawaii report recommends against nuclear development (2026-01-26)","url":"https://www.ans.org/news/2026-01-26/article-7698/hawaii-report-recommends-against-nuclear-development/","quote":"are neither commercially viable nor technically proven","status":"fetched"},{"label":"The Garden Island — Nuclear power exploration legislation gains steam (2025-02-19)","url":"https://www.thegardenisland.com/2025/02/19/hawaii-news/nuclear-power-exploration-legislation-gains-steam/","quote":"proposed to repeal a section of Hawaii's Constitution that requires two-thirds votes in both the House and Senate","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Kauai Now — Hawai'i pays 64% above national average for electricity (2025-03-06)","url":"https://kauainownews.com/2025/03/06/hawai%CA%BBi-pays-64-above-national-average-for-electricity/","quote":"rates on Kauaʻi range between 33 and 39 cents","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Honolulu Civil Beat — High Oil Prices Hammer HECO Customers. Here's Why (2026-07)","url":"https://www.civilbeat.org/2026/07/high-oil-prices-hammer-heco-customers-heres-why/","quote":"the average monthly residential bill rose 27% from June 2025 to this year on Maui and 30% on Oʻahu","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Hawaiian Electric — HELCO Energy Cost Recovery Clause filing (2025-02)","url":"https://www.hawaiianelectric.com/documents/billing_and_payment/rates/energy_cost_adjustment_filings/hawaii/2025/helco_ecrc_2025_02.pdf","quote":"Hawaii Electric Light: 35% HSFO and 65% Diesel; Maui Electric: 24% HSFO and 76% Diesel","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"cnmi-cuc","region":"Northern Mariana Islands (Saipan, Tinian, Rota)","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US territory","power_system":"The Commonwealth Utilities Corporation (CUC) runs diesel generation on Saipan, Tinian and Rota with 'near-total reliance on imported petroleum products for both electricity generation and transportation' and 'aging electricity infrastructure and vulnerability to natural disasters.' Saipan's plants (Power Plant 1 designed for 86 MW across eight units, Plant 2 at 10 MW, Plant 4 at 16 MW) were reported at 62.1 MW combined availability in March 2025; a 2026 typhoon cut that further, with a recovery update citing 42.5 MW restored on Saipan and Rota timelines still being set.","price":"US$0.19-0.33/kWh residential range, 2025 (DOE); CUC's own comparison put CNMI at US$0.2749/kWh residential in November 2024, called the lowest among US-affiliated Pacific jurisdictions versus $0.4139/kWh in Guam, $0.4414/kWh on Hawaii Island and $0.3986/kWh in American Samoa at that date. A separate Fuel Adjustment Charge of $0.21119/kWh took effect 2025-03-01; CUC's base rate has been unchanged since April 2014.","nuclear_position":"No CNMI nuclear ban found. Senator Jude Hofschneider pre-filed Senate Joint Resolution 24-05 supporting federal study, evaluation and possible future deployment of small modular reactors and microreactors on Saipan, Tinian or Rota, while the resolution itself concedes 'the CNMI does not have the financial capacity to independently fund the installation, operation, or long-term maintenance of such systems.' A CNMI House member has separately opposed nuclear technology in the territory in the context of the Department of Defense's Project JANUS, a distinct military proposal, per local reporting not independently fetched in this pass.","microreactor_activity":"SJR 24-05 is framed as civilian and federal-facing: it 'does not authorise construction of a nuclear facility' but 'calls for federal agencies to consider Saipan, Tinian and Rota for feasibility studies, pilot programmes or possible future deployment,' citing exposure to volatile imported-fuel prices and grid damage from a recent super typhoon. No funded federal study or agency response was found as of this pass.","microreactor_read":"A sitting CNMI senator has already put Saipan, Tinian and Rota on record asking the federal government to study microreactors, an unusually direct civilian ask among the territories, but the same resolution admits CNMI cannot pay for one itself, so any deal has to be federally funded or vendor-financed, not locally procured, and has to be kept visibly separate from the contested military reactor proposal next to it.","group":"us-islands","position":"study","price_short":"$0.27/kWh residential (2024)","loads":[{"name":"Saipan (CUC Power Plants 1, 2 and 4)","mw":"42.5 MW available (2026 post-typhoon recovery update), versus 62.1 MW combined availability in March 2025","note":"diesel; typhoon-damaged and under repair as of this pass"}],"blockers":["SJR 24-05 was pre-filed/introduced but had not, as of this pass, produced a funded federal study or agency commitment","The resolution's own text states CNMI lacks the financial capacity to fund installation, operation or long-term maintenance of any such system","Civilian nuclear interest (SJR 24-05) sits alongside a separate, opposed military reactor proposal (Project JANUS), risking public conflation of the two","2026 typhoon damage has CUC focused on restoring diesel capacity on Saipan and Rota rather than evaluating new generation types"],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Department of Energy Grid Deployment Office — Commonwealth of the Northern Mariana Islands (CNMI) (2025)","url":"https://www.energy.gov/oe/commonwealth-northern-mariana-islands-cnmi","quote":"Electricity costs for residential customers in the CNMI in 2025 ranged from $0.19 to $0.33 U.S. dollars per kilowatt-hour (kWh).","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Marianas Variety — CUC: NMI power rates lowest in US Pacific region (2024-11-28)","url":"https://www.mvariety.com/news/local/cuc-nmi-power-rates-lowest-in-us-pacific-region/article_aac108fc-accd-11ef-844a-939e3ec3ca67.html/","quote":"$27.49 residential and $32.42 commercial","status":"fetched"},{"label":"RNZ — CNMI lawmaker wants nuclear power study amid energy security concerns","url":"https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/pacific/597311/cnmi-lawmaker-wants-nuclear-power-study-amid-energy-security-concerns","quote":"the CNMI does not have the financial capacity to independently fund the installation, operation, or long-term maintenance of such systems","status":"snippet-only"},{"label":"NMI News Service — Bavi Recovery Rundown Day 10: Saipan Generation Jumps to 42.5 Megawatts as Rota Timelines Take Shape","url":"https://www.nminewsservice.com/bavi-recovery-rundown-day-10/","quote":"Saipan Generation Jumps to 42.5 Megawatts as Rota Timelines Take Shape","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"freely-associated-states-pacific","region":"Palau, Federated States of Micronesia and Marshall Islands","country":"PW/FM/MH","jurisdiction":"Compact of Free Association states (freely associated with the US)","power_system":"All three run small, diesel-dominated grids with no interconnection to any other network. Palau's Public Utilities Corporation (PPUC) operates the Malakal Power Plant (23.7 MW installed across 8 diesel generators) and the Aimeliik Power Plant (about 13.08 MW across 4 diesel generators), for roughly 28 MW installed capacity against a 15 MW peak load and a generation mix around 97.5% diesel / 2.5% solar. The Marshall Islands' Marshalls Energy Company (MEC) supplies Majuro (plus Jaluit and Wotje); its Majuro system, the country's largest, had a 2023 recorded maximum demand of about 9.8 MW (9.0 MW daily average), generated about 99.6% from diesel and 0.4% from grid-connected PV.","price":"US$0.28/kWh residential in Palau (rising to $0.41/kWh commercial/government on an inverted block tariff) and about US$0.48/kWh in the Federated States of Micronesia, per trade-press reporting; a separate estimate put Marshall Islands residential price at about US$0.36/kWh","nuclear_position":"No civil nuclear power position, statute or study was found for Palau, FSM or the Marshall Islands in this pass; all three are eligible to join, but have not signed, the South Pacific Nuclear Free Zone Treaty. The Marshall Islands carries a distinct, unrelated legacy of '67 atmospheric nuclear test explosions conducted between 1946 and 1958' at Bikini and Enewetak atolls, addressed by a US-RMI compensation MOU (fiscal year 2024) providing '$50 million annually,' '$200 million over 20 years for joint health care programs' and a '$700 million' repurposed trust fund, plus a UN nuclear-weapons-impact study launched in November 2024, a weapons-testing history that any civil microreactor outreach must approach with care and keep clearly separate from.","microreactor_activity":"none found for civil power generation in any of the three states, searched directly and via Pacific-region energy literature; the only nuclear-related activity found for any of them concerns weapons-testing legacy and compensation, not power generation","microreactor_read":"Palau and the FSM pay among the highest delivered-diesel prices found in this whole pass, but no state has studied nuclear power, every identified grid is small even for the bottom of the 1-20 MW range, and the Marshall Islands' weapons-testing history means outreach there needs deliberately careful, separate framing rather than a standard utility pitch.","group":"us-islands","position":"silent","price_short":"$0.28-0.48/kWh (Palau, FSM)","loads":[{"name":"Majuro (Marshalls Energy Company system)","mw":"about 9.8 MW maximum demand, 9.0 MW daily average (2023)","note":"diesel; the Marshall Islands' largest single grid"}],"blockers":["No feasibility study, MOU or utility statement on civil nuclear power found in any of the three states","Every grid found is small even for the bottom of the 1-20 MW range, Majuro's system, the largest identified, peaks under 10 MW","The Marshall Islands' weapons-testing legacy makes any nuclear-power conversation politically sensitive and requires framing kept distinct from compensation and testing-impact issues"],"sources":[{"label":"POWER Magazine — Marooned: How Island Power Systems Keep the Lights On","url":"https://www.powermag.com/marooned-how-island-power-systems-keep-the-lights-on/","quote":"Pacific island nations pay between $0.28/kWh (Palau) and $0.48/kWh (the Federated States of Micronesia)","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Wikipedia — Malakal Power Plant","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malakal_Power_Plant","quote":"Nameplate capacity 23.7 MW","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Arms Control Association — U.S., Marshall Islands Sign Deal on Nuclear Testing Impacts (2023-03)","url":"https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2023-03/news/us-marshall-islands-sign-deal-nuclear-testing-impacts","quote":"67 atmospheric nuclear test explosions conducted between 1946 and 1958","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Radio Free Asia — Pacific push sees UN launch first nuclear war impact study in 35 years (2024-11-04)","url":"https://www.rfa.org/english/pacific/2024/11/04/un-nuclear-impact-marshall-islands-french-polynesia/","quote":"UN launch first nuclear war impact study in 35 years","status":"snippet-only"},{"label":"Devex / PRDR Sustainable Energy for All — Palau Public Utilities Corporation (PPUC) profile","url":"https://prdrse4all.spc.int/content/palau-public-utilities-corporation-ppuc","quote":"installed generation capacity of 28.05 megawatts (MW) and peak load of 15 MW","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"puerto-rico","region":"Puerto Rico","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US territory","power_system":"PREPA owns the grid but no longer runs it: LUMA Energy has held the transmission-and-distribution O&M contract since June 2021, and Genera PR (a New Fortress Energy subsidiary) has run PREPA's legacy thermal generation fleet under a public-private O&M agreement since 2023, tasked to 'manage, operate, maintain and eventually decommission some of all the Legacy Generation Assets, base-load generation plants and peaking plants.' In the 12 months to April 2026 fossil fuels supplied about 97% of generation: natural gas 47%, coal about 17%, plus fuel oil, against solar 1.4% and wind 1.1%.","price":"US$0.26/kWh residential, effective 2026-01-01 (a $0.012739/kWh cut from the prior quarter's fuel and purchased-power adjustment)","nuclear_position":"No legal ban on nuclear power. Puerto Rico's legislature passed a 2018 law directing a study of nuclear feasibility for the island. A Puerto Rico House bill on nuclear-energy research was reported under committee review in January 2025.","microreactor_activity":"The Nuclear Alternative Project (NAP), a Puerto Rico-based nonprofit founded in 2016, obtained DOE Office of Nuclear Energy sponsorship for a Preliminary Feasibility Study for SMRs and microreactors, carried out via Idaho National Laboratory (findings released May 2020), which concluded advanced reactors 'can meet Puerto Rico's unique energy needs by complementing renewable sources with zero-emission electricity resilient to extreme natural events.' In 2021 DOE awarded NAP about $1.6 million for a site-suitability phase, which named two candidate sites, one lying 'on the island's north coast near an industrial hub' and the other 'on its east coast at the former Roosevelt Roads Naval Station' (a decommissioned base, not an active military installation). NAP also reported a commissioned survey of over 3,000 residents finding 94% interested in continuing to explore nuclear energy. No reactor vendor, NRC license application or in-service date had been announced as of this pass.","microreactor_read":"The DOE-funded feasibility case is the most advanced of any US island jurisdiction, with two named candidate sites and a favorable resident survey, but nobody has converted it into an RFP, an NRC docket or a signed PPA counterparty since 2021, the buyer-side vacuum, not the technical case, is what is missing.","group":"us-islands","position":"study","price_short":"$0.26/kWh residential (2026)","loads":[],"blockers":["No reactor vendor selected and no NRC license application filed as of this pass","Generation is split between LUMA (transmission and distribution) and Genera (legacy thermal generation), so no single utility counterparty yet owns a nuclear procurement decision","2025-2026 regulatory attention is on emergency thermal procurement (Genera's request to acquire 700-850 MW of temporary generation) rather than nuclear"],"sources":[{"label":"San Juan Daily Star — Regulator approves power rate reduction effective in January (2026)","url":"https://www.sanjuandailystar.com/post/regulator-approves-power-rate-reduction-effective-in-january","quote":"From Jan. 1 through March 31, the cost per kWh will be $0.26.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"World Nuclear News — Small reactors feasible in Puerto Rico, study concludes (2020)","url":"https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Small-reactors-feasible-in-Puerto-Rico,-study-conc","quote":"Advanced nuclear reactors can meet Puerto Rico's unique energy needs by complementing renewable sources with zero-emission electricity resilient to extreme natural events","status":"fetched"},{"label":"World Nuclear News — Site analysis proposed for Puerto Rico (2021)","url":"https://world-nuclear-news.org/Articles/Site-analysis-in-progress-for-Puerto-Rico","quote":"One lies on the island's north coast near an industrial hub; the other on its east coast at the former Roosevelt Roads Naval Station.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Low-Carbon Power — Puerto Rico electricity generation mix (May 2025-Apr 2026)","url":"https://lowcarbonpower.org/region/Puerto_Rico","quote":"natural gas alone was responsible for almost half of the electricity supply, specifically 47%","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Puerto Rico Public-Private Partnerships Authority — Thermal Generation Facilities (Genera PR)","url":"https://www.p3.pr.gov/existing-p3/thermal-generation-facilities","quote":"Manage, operate, maintain and eventually decommission some of all the Legacy Generation Assets, base-load generation plants and peaking plants.","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"us-virgin-islands","region":"U.S. Virgin Islands","country":"US","jurisdiction":"US territory","power_system":"WAPA (Virgin Islands Water and Power Authority) runs two separate island grids on delivered fuel: St. Thomas/St. John and St. Croix, with 238 MW of installed capacity as of 2020 (138 MW St. Thomas district, 100 MW St. Croix), historically burning a propane/diesel mix (74% propane, 26% diesel in March 2020). WAPA's Phase II Wartsila project, four new dual-fuel engines able to run on LPG or light fuel oil, rated at 36 MW and able to 'power 55% of the St. Thomas/St. John district', reached completion at a ribbon-cutting on 2025-01-17.","price":"US$0.42/kWh average residential price, 2025 (DOE); the fuel-cost LEAC component was set at 22.22 cents/kWh through 2026-06-30 under a 2025 WAPA-PSC settlement","nuclear_position":"No operating reactors or fuel-cycle facilities are sited in the territory. No nuclear-related statement, study or bill was found from WAPA, the Virgin Islands Energy Office, or the territorial legislature in this search pass.","microreactor_activity":"none found, searched WAPA's own site, DOE's territory energy-office page and NRC's state finder; no microreactor MOU, feasibility study or vendor visit turned up","microreactor_read":"USVI pays a delivered-fuel retail price (42 cents/kWh) close to Puerto Rico's, but with zero feasibility work, MOUs or legislative interest on record, so a microreactor sale here starts years behind Puerto Rico rather than riding alongside it.","group":"us-islands","position":"silent","price_short":"$0.42/kWh residential (2025)","loads":[{"name":"St. Thomas/St. John district (Randolph Harley Power Plant, Phase II Wartsila units)","mw":"36 MW new capacity (within a 138 MW St. Thomas-district total, 2020 figure)","note":"dual-fuel propane/light-fuel-oil; completed January 2025"}],"blockers":["No named nuclear feasibility study, MOU or vendor interest to build on, unlike Puerto Rico","WAPA's 2023-2025 capital program is already committed to new propane-capable thermal units (Wartsila Phase II)","Two disconnected island grids (St. Thomas/St. John and St. Croix) mean a single unit serves only part of the territory's ~238 MW system"],"sources":[{"label":"U.S. Department of Energy Grid Deployment Office — U.S. Virgin Islands (2025)","url":"https://www.energy.gov/oe/us-virgin-islands","quote":"In 2025, the average electricity price for residential customers in the U.S. Virgin Islands was approximately $0.42/kWh.","status":"fetched"},{"label":"V.I. Office of Disaster Recovery — WAPA Celebrates Ribbon-Cutting Ceremony for Phase II Wartsila Project (Jan. 17, 2025)","url":"https://www.usviodr.com/wapa-celebrates-with-ribbon-cutting-ceremony-for-phase-ii-wartsila-project/","quote":"power 55% of the St. Thomas/St. John district","status":"fetched"},{"label":"St. Croix Source — New WAPA Generators Will Mean 100 Percent Propane, Zero Fuel Oil (2020)","url":"https://stcroixsource.com/2020/05/01/new-wapa-generators-will-mean-100-percent-propane-zero-fuel-oil/","quote":"the consumption ratio at WAPA power plants was 74 percent propane and 26 percent diesel fuel","status":"snippet-only"},{"label":"NRC — Info Finder: Virgin Islands","url":"https://www.nrc.gov/info-finder/region-state/virgin-islands","quote":"no operating nuclear reactors or fuel cycle facilities are currently located in the U.S. Virgin Islands","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"australia-nuclear-legal-position","region":"Australia (federal and state nuclear law)","country":"AU","jurisdiction":"Australian Commonwealth (federal) plus five states","power_system":"Australia's National Electricity Market has no civil nuclear generation; the country's only reactor is ANSTO's OPAL research reactor at Lucas Heights, not a power plant. The opposition Coalition's 2024-2025 election platform proposed a taxpayer-funded fleet of seven nuclear power stations costing about A$331 billion (reported as roughly US$211 billion), with the first plant targeted for 2035-37.","nuclear_position":"Nuclear power generation is banned by two Commonwealth statutes: the Australian Radiation Protection and Nuclear Safety Act 1998 and the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act 1999, which could be lifted federally only by deleting the phrase \"a nuclear power plant\" from sections 37J, 140A, 146M and 305(2)(d) of the EPBC Act (and the equivalent ARPANS Act section). Because onshore generation is a state matter, New South Wales, Queensland, Victoria, South Australia and Western Australia each maintain their own separate nuclear bans that would also need repeal; Queensland's law separately requires a public referendum before Commonwealth-backed nuclear construction there. The Coalition took its 7-reactor plan to the May 2025 federal election and lost; energy analyst Tony Wood said within hours that 'the plan they took to the election is dead,' though the Coalition/Nationals remain split between abandoning nuclear and simply removing the ban without a taxpayer-funded build. A 23 September 2026 Coalition policy announcement still listed lifting the federal nuclear prohibition among an eight-point 'within 30 days of getting into government' package, which the reporting itself notes 'there is no chance for the Coalition to win support without either Labor or the Greens changing positions on nuclear energy' in the Senate.","microreactor_activity":"No microreactor-specific (1-20 MW) study, MOU or legislative carve-out was found; all Australian nuclear-power debate located in this pass concerns the Coalition's large, roughly gigawatt-class reactor fleet, not microreactors. The clearest AUKUS-driven civil-adjacent development is regulatory and workforce buildout: the Australian Naval Nuclear Power Safety Regulator (ANNPSR), a new statutory agency launched 2025-11-01 'commencing with over 70 trained staff' to oversee naval nuclear propulsion, with ARPANSA stating that it and ANNPSR 'will work together to support consistent nuclear and radiological protection across Australia's military and civilian activities.' This is a nuclear-regulatory workforce and capability build-out with potential civil spillover, not a civil licensing pathway.","microreactor_read":"Every part of Australia's nuclear debate, the federal ban, the five state bans, the Coalition's costed fleet plan, and the new AUKUS naval regulator, is built around large power reactors or naval propulsion, not the 1-20 MW class; a microreactor seller has no legal pathway federally or in any state today, and the nearest asset this creates is a slowly growing, civilian-adjacent nuclear regulatory workforce (ANNPSR/ARPANSA) rather than any change in the law itself.","group":"australia","position":"banned","price_short":"federal and state law","loads":[],"blockers":["Nuclear power generation is banned by name in both the ARPANS Act and the EPBC Act at the federal level","Onshore generation is constitutionally a state matter, so five states with their own nuclear bans (NSW, Queensland, Victoria, South Australia, Western Australia) would each need separate repeal even after any federal change","Queensland's law separately requires a public referendum before Commonwealth-backed nuclear construction there","The Coalition lost the May 2025 election on a platform that included a large nuclear-fleet plan, and lacks the Senate numbers to repeal the federal ban even from opposition","All political, legal and regulatory attention found is on large (roughly 1 GW-class) power reactors or naval propulsion, not the 1-20 MW microreactor class this record covers"],"sources":[{"label":"Clayton Utz — A nuclear powered Australia? (2025-03)","url":"https://www.claytonutz.com/insights/2025/march/a-nuclear-powered-australia","quote":"\"a nuclear power plant\" from sections 37J, 140A, 146M and 305(2)(d) of the EPBC Act","status":"fetched"},{"label":"ABC News — The election result makes the nuclear fight even tougher for the Coalition (2025-05-08)","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-08/nuclear-climate-election-campaign-coalition-nationals/105261046","quote":"the plan they took to the election is dead","status":"fetched"},{"label":"ABC News — Coalition vows to 'take a chainsaw' to climate laws and offshore wind (2026-09-23)","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-23/coalition-energy-plan-scrap-renewables-climate-laws/107184376","quote":"there is no chance for the Coalition to win support without either Labor or the Greens changing positions on nuclear energy","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Naval News — AUKUS: Launch of the Australian Naval Nuclear Power Safety Regulator (2025-11-02)","url":"https://www.navalnews.com/event-news/indo-pacific-2025/2025/11/aukus-launch-of-the-australian-naval-nuclear-power-safety-regulator/","quote":"the two Regulators will work together to support consistent nuclear and radiological safety across Australia’s military and civilian activities","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Bloomberg — Australia's Opposition Reveals $211 Billion Nuclear Plan (2024-12-13)","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-12-13/australia-s-opposition-reveals-211-billion-nuclear-power-plan","quote":"Australia's Opposition Reveals $211 Billion Nuclear Plan","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"australia-remote-power-market","region":"Australia: Western Australia and Northern Territory off-grid and remote power","country":"AU","jurisdiction":"Australian state (Western Australia) and territory (Northern Territory)","power_system":"Horizon Power, WA's regional/remote state-owned utility, runs 34 microgrids supplying about 100,000 residents and 10,000 businesses, and is rolling out standalone solar-battery power systems to remote Aboriginal communities under a state-funded 'ReGen' program; its Midwest Centralised Solar Project is adding solar-plus-battery to six towns (Cue, Meekatharra, Sandstone, Wiluna, Yalgoo and Norseman). In the Northern Territory, Power and Water Corporation's not-for-profit subsidiary Indigenous Essential Services runs 51 diesel-fired power stations (about 80 MW installed) across 72 remote communities and 66 outstations under a Uniform Tariff Policy with a Community Service Obligation subsidy; its ARENA-funded Solar Energy Transformation Program (SETuP) added 10 MW of solar microgrids across 25 communities, saving an estimated 94 million litres of diesel over the program's 25-year life. Separately, 'more than 100 mines (out of a total of 350)' in Australia run fully off-grid, concentrated in WA's Pilbara and Goldfields, with large hybrid renewable-plus-gas microgrids now built at sites including Agnew (EDL/Gold Fields), Tropicana (Pacific Energy), Kathleen Valley (lithium) and BHP and Rio Tinto operations.","price":"WA off-grid mine diesel generation has historically cost upwards of $0.50/kWh, with recent hybrid-displaced costs reported around $0.25-0.35/kWh; Horizon Power's ReGen rollout is backed by an A$27 million (about US$17.9 million) state-government commitment and is displacing diesel volumes such as 35,000 litres/year at Cockatoo Springs and 15,000 litres/year at Chile Creek","nuclear_position":"Covered at the national level by this pass's australia-nuclear-legal-position record: nuclear generation is banned under Commonwealth law and under Western Australian and Northern Territory law alike. Neither Horizon Power nor Power and Water/Indigenous Essential Services has taken a public position on nuclear power for their remote systems.","microreactor_activity":"Industry advocacy rather than utility action: the Australian Nuclear Association's 2025 conference included a paper, 'The Case for Microreactors in Australia' (Irwin, ANA2025), and the advocacy group Nuclear for Australia argued in 2026 that remote mines, communities and data centres are candidate markets, citing the 2026-06-04 criticality of Antares Nuclear's Mark-0 microreactor (100 kW-1 MW) at Idaho National Laboratory as a milestone that 'highlights emerging opportunities for Australia.' No Horizon Power, Power and Water/IES, or WA/NT government microreactor study, RFI or MOU was found.","microreactor_read":"WA and the NT have exactly the diesel-cost profile (historically $0.50+/kWh, still $0.25-0.35/kWh even after hybridisation) and the off-grid scale (100+ mines, dozens of remote-community microgrids) that a 1-20 MW reactor should target, but a state and federal nuclear ban forecloses the market entirely for now, and the utilities and mine operators who would buy have already committed capital to solar-battery-gas hybrids, microreactor advocacy here is still at the conference-paper stage, not a funded study.","group":"australia","position":"banned","price_short":"$0.25-0.50/kWh off-grid mines (WA)","loads":[{"name":"Indigenous Essential Services remote NT grids","mw":"about 80 MW installed across 51 diesel power stations","note":"72 remote communities plus 66 outstations; diesel, partially solar-offset by the 10 MW SETuP program"},{"name":"Agnew Gold Mine (Gold Fields/EDL)","mw":"18 MW wind + 4 MW solar + 13 MW/4 MWh battery + 16 MW gas","note":"off-grid hybrid microgrid, forecast up to 60% renewable"}],"blockers":["Nuclear power is banned under both Commonwealth law and Western Australian/Northern Territory law (see australia-nuclear-legal-position)","Horizon Power and Indigenous Essential Services are both already mid-rollout on solar-plus-battery and standalone power system programs (ReGen, SETuP, Midwest Centralised Solar) that are the funded, near-term alternative to diesel","Off-grid mine power is dominated by large, well-capitalised hybrid renewable-plus-gas microgrids (Agnew, Tropicana, Kathleen Valley) built by independent power producers on 10-15 year terms, which a reactor would have to displace or out-price","All identified microreactor interest in Australia is advocacy-stage (conference papers, industry commentary), with no utility, mine operator or government study found"],"sources":[{"label":"pv magazine Australia — Horizon rolls out renewable energy solutions for remote communities (2025-12-09)","url":"https://www.pv-magazine-australia.com/2025/12/09/horizon-rolls-out-renewable-energy-solutions-for-remote-communities/","quote":"$27 million (USD 17.9 million)","status":"fetched"},{"label":"ARENA — Agnew Renewable Energy Microgrid project page","url":"https://arena.gov.au/projects/agnew-renewable-energy-microgrid/","quote":"forecast to deliver up to 60% renewable energy to the Agnew mine","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Mining Technology — Going off-grid: the state of on-site power at Australian mines","url":"https://www.mining-technology.com/features/off-grid-on-site-power-australian-mines/","quote":"more than 100 mines (out of a total of 350)","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Nuclear for Australia — US Microreactor Milestone Creates New Nuclear Opportunity for Australia (2026)","url":"https://www.nuclearforaustralia.com/u_s_microreactor_milestone_highlights_emerging_opportunities_for_australia","quote":"between 100 kW and 1 MW of electricity","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Power and Water Corporation — Remote power sources","url":"https://www.powerwater.com.au/about/what-we-do/remote-operations/remote-power-sources","quote":"72 remote communities and 66 outstations ... 51 diesel-fired power stations ... about 80 megawatts","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"uk-overseas-territories-south-atlantic","region":"Falkland Islands, St Helena and Tristan da Cunha","country":"GB","jurisdiction":"UK overseas territory","power_system":"Stanley, the Falklands' capital, runs on diesel plus six 330 kW Enercon wind turbines (the last three commissioned February 2010); the turbines met a then-record '40% of the total electricity required by Stanley' in November 2010, with weekly diesel displacement as high as 30,290 litres. A Sand Bay Wind Farm Phase Three, approved December 2023, is expected to raise Stanley's 'annual renewable contribution to over 50%... including periods with 100% renewable energy provision until the mid-2040s.' St Helena's grid (Connect Saint Helena Ltd) is 'now averaging around 30% renewable contribution,' backed by the Half Tree Hollow Solar Farm and battery storage, against a stated goal of 'a minimum of 85% renewable energy generation.' Tristan da Cunha, population about 250, the most remote inhabited island in this record, runs a small diesel system rebuilt after a fire, supplemented since 2015-04-30 by a pilot 6.5 kW solar array ('26 solar panels ... each capable of generating 250 watts').","price":"St Helena electricity cost 'up to £0.42 per kWh' depending on consumption, 2014 (most recent figure found); a Falklands government order cut the 'price of electricity per unit for all consumers... from £23 to £18' effective 2020-05-01 (unit basis not confirmed as standard kWh in this pass)","nuclear_position":"No civil nuclear power study, statement or interest was found for the Falkland Islands, St Helena or Tristan da Cunha in this pass; each territory's own energy-strategy documents discuss only diesel, wind, solar and battery options. The Falklands government's energy strategy leaves the door open in general terms only: 'technology is evolving at pace, and options that are currently unfeasible may become feasible in future, whereas others may become obsolete or less attractive', a generic statement about energy technology, not a nuclear-specific one.","microreactor_activity":"none found for any of the three territories, searched directly; no MOU, feasibility study or vendor visit specific to nuclear power turned up for the Falklands, St Helena or Tristan da Cunha","microreactor_read":"All three pay a real diesel premium on small, remote grids, but each already has a funded renewables-plus-storage roadmap in progress and none shows any nuclear interest, Stanley's own wind build-out is explicitly planned to reach 100% renewable for periods of the year by the mid-2040s, which is the incumbent a microreactor pitch would have to unseat rather than simply replace diesel.","group":"uk-allies","position":"silent","price_short":"up to £0.42/kWh (St Helena, 2014)","loads":[{"name":"Stanley (Falkland Islands capital)","mw":"six 330 kW wind turbines (about 2 MW) plus diesel","note":"wind met a record 40% of Stanley's electricity in Nov 2010; Sand Bay Phase Three (approved Dec 2023) targets over 50% annual renewable"},{"name":"Tristan da Cunha","mw":"6.5 kW pilot solar array (26 panels) plus diesel","note":"population about 250; far below microreactor scale"}],"blockers":["No feasibility study, MOU or government statement on nuclear power found for any of the three territories","All three already have funded renewables-plus-diesel roadmaps in progress (Falklands' Sand Bay wind phases, St Helena's 85% renewable target, Tristan's solar pilot)","Tristan da Cunha's population (about 250) and grid scale (kW, not MW) put it far below any plausible microreactor market; the Falklands (Stanley) and St Helena are the only two of the three with grids that could conceivably approach the 1-20 MW range"],"sources":[{"label":"MercoPress — Falklands' wind power contribution new record: 40% of Stanley's consumption (2010-11-15)","url":"https://en.mercopress.com/2010/11/15/falklands-wind-power-contribution-new-record-40-of-stanley-s-consumption","quote":"40% of the total electricity required by Stanley","status":"fetched"},{"label":"MercoPress — Falklands will proceed with Phase Three of Sand Bay Wind Farm (2023-12-06)","url":"https://en.mercopress.com/2023/12/06/falklands-will-proceed-with-phase-three-of-sand-bay-wind-farm","quote":"increase the annual renewable contribution to over 50% for Stanley, including periods with 100% renewable energy provision until the mid-2040s","status":"fetched"},{"label":"St Helena Government — Renewable Energy","url":"https://www.sainthelena.gov.sh/renewable-energy/","quote":"now averaging around 30% renewable contribution to the generation of electricity","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Tristan da Cunha Development and Public Works — Tristan da Cunha Renewable Energy Project","url":"https://www.tristandc.com/renewableenergy.php","quote":"26 solar panels, aligned to face the northern midday sun and each capable of generating 250 watts","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Saint Helena Island Info — Renewable Energy","url":"https://sainthelenaisland.info/renewableenergy.htm","quote":"in 2014 it was up to £0.42p per KwH, depending on consumption","status":"fetched"}]},{"id":"nz-chatham-islands-and-nuclear-law","region":"New Zealand: Chatham Islands and national nuclear-free legislation","country":"NZ","jurisdiction":"New Zealand (Chatham Islands, a special local-government area)","power_system":"The Chatham Islands sit roughly 800 km east of Christchurch with no connection to New Zealand's national grid, so the main settlement has run overwhelmingly on diesel. A community wind-turbine project 'began operation in November' 2025 and is 'expected to reduce diesel use by up to 68%, and save more than $1 million a year,' with grid power for islanders 'expected to drop by 20%.'","price":"NZ$1.32/kWh from 2026-06-01 (a 32% rise), 'more than three times the mainland average of about 40 cents'; islanders had already been paying $1.02/kWh in 2020 versus about 25c on the mainland","nuclear_position":"New Zealand's Nuclear Free Zone, Disarmament, and Arms Control Act 1987 is widely assumed to ban nuclear power outright, but per the World Nuclear Association, 'The New Zealand Nuclear Free Zone established under the Act does not ban land-based nuclear power plants', its actual prohibitions target nuclear weapons and nuclear-propelled vessels entering New Zealand's territorial sea, land and airspace, not civilian electricity generation. No national or Chatham Islands-specific law bans a civil reactor, but no government body has proposed one either.","microreactor_activity":"none found from any New Zealand government body, nationally or for the Chathams specifically. The only advocacy found was an August 2025 opinion piece calling nuclear 'the last swear word in New Zealand politics' and urging 'a feasibility study to work out the who, when, and where of SMRs', itself confirming no such study yet exists.","microreactor_read":"The Chathams pay the highest, most volatile delivered-diesel price found in this whole record (NZ$1.32/kWh, up 32% in a year even after new wind capacity), and contrary to popular belief no New Zealand law actually bans a land-based reactor, but the political taboo is doing the work the law does not, and the islands' own funded fix (new wind turbines, targeting a 68% diesel cut) is already delivering savings.","group":"uk-allies","position":"silent","price_short":"NZ$1.32/kWh (2026)","loads":[{"name":"Chatham Islands (Waitangi main grid)","mw":"not stated in MW in this pass; diesel plus new wind turbines (operational November 2025)","note":"off-grid, no connection to the NZ national grid; price rose 32% to NZ$1.32/kWh from June 2026 despite the new wind capacity"}],"blockers":["No government feasibility study, RFI or MOU on nuclear power exists at the national or Chatham Islands level","Nuclear power remains politically described as 'the last swear word in New Zealand politics' even though it is not actually illegal for land-based generation","The Chathams' near-term answer to diesel cost is already underway and partly delivered: a community wind-turbine project operating since November 2025, targeting a 68% diesel-use cut"],"sources":[{"label":"NEWS WIRE — Chatham Islands power price rises 32 percent to $1.32 a kilowatt hour from June (2026-05)","url":"https://newswire.co.nz/2026/05/chatham-islands-power-price-rises-32-percent-to-1-32-a-kilowatt-hour-from-june-more-than-three-times-the-mainland-average/","quote":"more than three times the mainland average of about 40 cents","status":"fetched"},{"label":"1News — Wind turbines bring hope of cheaper power to the Chatham Islands (2025-12-23)","url":"https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/12/23/wind-turbines-bring-hope-of-cheaper-power-to-the-chatham-islands/","quote":"expected to reduce diesel use by up to 68%, and save more than $1 million a year","status":"fetched"},{"label":"World Nuclear Association — Nuclear Energy Prospects in New Zealand","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/country-profiles/countries-g-n/new-zealand","quote":"The New Zealand Nuclear Free Zone established under the Act does not ban land-based nuclear power plants","status":"fetched"},{"label":"1News — Dissolving the nuclear taboo would benefit NZ hugely – but do we have the guts? (2025-08-19)","url":"https://www.1news.co.nz/2025/08/19/dissolving-the-nuclear-taboo-would-benefit-nz-hugely-but-do-we-have-the-guts/","quote":"a feasibility study to work out the who, when, and where of SMRs","status":"fetched"},{"label":"RNZ — Chatham Islands braces for energy shock","url":"https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/589190/chatham-islands-braces-for-energy-shock","quote":"Power cost 129.5c/kWh -- among the highest in New Zealand","status":"snippet-only"}]},{"id":"uk-scottish-islands-and-nuclear-framework","region":"UK: Scottish islands and the UK advanced nuclear/microreactor framework","country":"GB","jurisdiction":"UK nation (Scotland, devolved) plus UK central government","power_system":"Shetland's grid 'is not synchronised to the national grid on mainland Scotland' and runs on local generation: Lerwick Power Station is 'the principal source of electrical energy for Shetland,' supplemented by about 20 MW from the gas-fired Sullom Voe Terminal power station (as of 2015) and community renewables. The 103-turbine Viking windfarm became operational on 1 September 2024, adding about 443,000 kW (443 MW) of installed renewable capacity, and a 68 MW lithium grid battery is scheduled for 2026. SSEN Transmission's 600 MW HVDC Shetland link is under construction, and a further 1.8 GW HVDC Western Isles link has been proposed, both meant to connect these island grids to the GB mainland system.","nuclear_position":"Scotland's devolved government (SNP-led since 2007) runs what is widely described as a 'no new nuclear power strategy,' opposing new nuclear stations on environmental, safety and cost grounds; the Scottish Parliament voted 63-58 to support that policy, and Scottish ministers use devolved planning powers to block proposed nuclear sites even though nuclear licensing itself is reserved to the UK government. This is a live 2026 election issue: Scottish Labour leader Anas Sarwar has vowed to end the SNP's 'ideological and anti-science' planning opposition, and a UK Government study was reported in mid-2026 to have identified Scottish sites with technical potential for new nuclear. At UK level, a March-2026 Advanced Nuclear Framework and Advanced Nuclear Pipeline (run by the Department for Energy Security & Net Zero with Great British Energy-Nuclear) opened a route for privately led SMR/AMR/microreactor projects to seek 'in-principle government endorsement.' On 2025-09-15 the UK and US signed the Atlantic Partnership for Advanced Nuclear Energy, under which 'if a reactor has already passed rigorous safety checks in one country, this work can be used by the other to support its assessment,' cutting expected licensing time 'from about three or four years to around two years.'","microreactor_activity":"Urenco and Radiant Energy (a US microreactor developer) signed a fuel-related agreement worth about GBP4 million around the September 2025 UK-US nuclear deals; X-Energy/Centrica (Hartlepool, 12 planned advanced modular reactors, about 2,500 jobs) and Holtec/EDF/Tritax (Cottam, Nottinghamshire, SMRs at a former coal station) were named under the Advanced Nuclear Pipeline. Separate trade reporting describes Last Energy pursuing a Nuclear Site Licence route for microreactors in Wales and MoltexFLEX targeting West Burton, both without a full Generic Design Assessment. No project specifically sited in Shetland, Orkney or the Western Isles was found; the islands' own near-term generation plan is windfarm-plus-HVDC-interconnection (Viking, the Shetland and Western Isles HVDC links, battery storage), not nuclear.","microreactor_read":"The UK now has two of the fastest private and government-endorsed routes to market for microreactors anywhere (the Advanced Nuclear Pipeline and the UK-US Atlantic Partnership's mutual safety-assessment recognition), but none of that machinery has yet pointed at Scotland's actual off-grid islands, which are instead already mid-build on a windfarm-plus-HVDC-cable answer to the same diesel-displacement problem, and any Scottish site would still run into the devolved government's own planning-level opposition to nuclear.","group":"uk-allies","position":"restricted","price_short":"tariff not fetched","loads":[],"blockers":["Scotland's devolved government has an active planning-level policy against new nuclear stations, enforced through consenting powers even though nuclear licensing is a reserved UK matter","The islands' funded near-term plan is renewables-plus-HVDC-interconnection (Viking windfarm, Shetland and Western Isles HVDC links, battery storage), not new generation of any other kind","No microreactor developer, study or MOU was found specifically targeting Shetland, Orkney or the Western Isles"],"sources":[{"label":"Wikipedia — Lerwick Power Station","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lerwick_Power_Station","quote":"is not synchronised to the national grid on mainland Scotland","status":"fetched"},{"label":"World Nuclear News — UK seeking pipeline of advanced nuclear projects","url":"https://www.world-nuclear-news.org/articles/uk-seeking-pipeline-of-advanced-nuclear-projects","quote":"plans for X-Energy and Centrica to build 12 advanced modular reactors in Hartlepool, supporting 2,500 jobs","status":"fetched"},{"label":"NucNet — UK And US Announce Major Partnership In New 'Golden Age' Of Nuclear Power Ahead Of Trump Visit (2025-09)","url":"https://www.nucnet.org/news/uk-and-us-announce-major-partnership-in-new-golden-age-of-nuclear-power-ahead-of-trump-visit-9-1-2025","quote":"if a reactor has already passed rigorous safety checks in one country, this work can be used by the other to support its assessment","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Shetland Islands Council — Shetland statistics: energy","url":"https://www.shetland.gov.uk/shetland-statistics/energy","quote":"the 103 turbine Viking windfarm becoming operational","status":"fetched"},{"label":"Energy Voice — Labour and SNP clash over nuclear power for Scotland amid Holyrood campaign","url":"https://www.energyvoice.com/renewables-energy-transition/nuclear/595535/labour-and-snp-clash-over-nuclear-power-for-scotland-amid-holyrood-campaign/","quote":"ideological and anti-science","status":"snippet-only"}]}]};window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('mr:regions'));
